            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

AIR SEALING



During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Some power outlets on exterior walls. You can add foam gaskets behind the faceplate to stop this.



** Around the back door. Consider replacing weather stripping.



** The ductwork for the furnace is allowing air infiltration. This could be due to rips in the belly bag, as there is venting connected to the belly bag.



** Signifcant air leakage is coming in through the hole in the bedroom wall (old cable outlet) and through the plumbing access hatch for the ensuite bathroom, also located in the bedroom. For the access hatch you can get a product called Draft Attack that allows for removable caulking in case you ever need access to the plumbing.



** Around the water heater. After investigating the belly bag I believe there's a rip here that could be repaired.



** Some of the windows are leaky, around the the wooden frames.



** Another likely source of air leakage is along the rear wall of your home. After investigating the crawl space/ belly bag I discovered the belly bag is not sealed along the back wall at all, which is most likely the cause of the cold floors you described in the bathroom.



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a 20% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $160 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 6.5 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year, and be more comfortable in your home.



The Government of Canada is offering funding towards air sealing, through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, and this funding can be used in combination with any AEA funding. Please see their web page for details about this program:



https://www.nrcan.gc.ca/energy-efficiency/homes/canada-greener-homes-grant/23441



-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

MAIN WALLS



The main walls of your home appear to be 2x6 construction with batt insulation (R20). I have modelled adding 2 additional inches (R10) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation (XPS) to the exterior (total R30). Two inches of XPS is likely the maximum thickness you could consider given the overhang of your roof. Due to the large amount of labour involved in adding insulation to the exterior of your home, and the high cost, this recommendation should only be taken in conjunction with a siding upgrade, and if you plan to stay in the home for a longer period of time.



Savings: By adding 2 inches (R10) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls, you could potentially save $300 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 12 GJ, and qualify for up to a $1,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



Based on our discussions, you mentioned you won't likely do an insulation upgrade on your home, but if you change your mind please contact me because there may be other funding available from AEA, in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



The Government of Canada is also offering funding towards insulation, through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, and this funding can be used in combination with AEA funding. Please see their web page for details about this program:



https://www.nrcan.gc.ca/energy-efficiency/homes/canada-greener-homes-grant/23441



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</mainWalls>
                <windows>

WINDOWS



All of the windows in this house (7 total) are older triple pane, wood windows, with some showing a need for repair. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows. I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $80 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.3 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



There is however, funding available from The Government of Canada for window upgrades, through the Canada Greener Homes Grant. Please see their web page for details about this program:



https://www.nrcan.gc.ca/energy-efficiency/homes/canada-greener-homes-grant/23441



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------







</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

PROPANE Instantaneous Upgrade from Electric



You currently have a 173 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. You may want to consider replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $1300 per year in energy costs, slightly increase your annual energy usage by 0.3 GJ,  and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

PROPANE FURNACE



Your house is currently heated with a newer 80% efficient propane furnace. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 95% AFUE condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $600 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 22 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



I know that it's very unlikely that you will upgrade your furnace, as you recently installed your current furnace, but I wanted to show you the difference that the efficiency makes to your fuel bill. You can apply this same technique to any other appliance upgrade you might consider doing. 



I've also modelled the use of your pellet stove to provide a significant portion of your heating. By using the pellet stove to provide an estimated 50% of the heating load for your home and using the furnace fan to circulate heat you are saving approximately $690 per year in heating costs, at current prices.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

ADDITIONAL COMMENTS





LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:   

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2020 values (updated each Fall, annually), including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $0.91 per liter 

Propane - $0.61 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



